HST Office: the HST "coat", every dialog's controls drawn in the HST look from the design tokens.

- Push buttons, input fields, spin fields, combo and list boxes (with their drop-down and spin buttons),
  check boxes, radio buttons, tab controls and scroll bars are drawn with the tokens of
  <vcl/hst/designtokens.hxx>: rounded corners, token borders, an accent focus border, hover/pressed/disabled
  fills, an accent check mark and radio dot, a pill-shaped scroll thumb. A tab control's tab row and pages are
  one continuous surface in the control's own background colour (a notebook bar's tint too) over the full width,
  the pages outlined, the selected tab marked by an accent bar. Light and dark palettes follow the theme. No .ui file changes.
- One hook in OutputDevice::DrawNativeControl hands each request to hst::coat first. The coat draws a part
  only where the platform already draws it natively (Windows, macOS; the VCL-drawn controls of gtk3); every
  size and content region still comes from the platform, so all layouts stay as they were.
- gtk3: the GTK-drawn (welded) dialogs get a GTK CSS provider generated from the same tokens, above the theme
  and below the user's gtk.css. It sets colours, radii and focus marks only, never sizes.
- Off in high-contrast mode, and when org.openoffice.Office.HSTAppearance/UiCoat/Enabled is false or
  HST_UI_COAT=0 (read once at start-up).
- CppunitTest_vcl_hst_coat probes the pixels of every covered control and state on a VirtualDevice, and checks
  the high-contrast and setting switches and the generated CSS.

